1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,030
The fastest useful way to compare player
prop odds is not

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:05,740
to scan for the biggest number.

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:07,960
It is to compare the same question,

4
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:12,050
captured at nearly the same time, and keep
the line separate

5
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:13,720
from the price or payout.

6
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:18,110
In this walkthrough, I will show you a
desktop workflow that

7
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:21,190
takes a few minutes and still leaves room
for a no-action

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,250
result. Start by opening each platform in
its own tab.

9
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:29,080
Then create one simple research row with
six fields:

10
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:33,200
player, stat, direction, exact line,
source, and timestamp.

11
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:35,560
Do not add an opinion yet.

12
00:00:35,980 --> 00:00:38,850
If one tab shows over eight and a half
rebounds and

13
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:40,860
another shows over nine and a half,

14
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,440
those are different questions.

15
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:44,440
Keep them on separate rows.

16
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:48,540
Next, record the terms attached to each
exact line.

17
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:52,270
On a sportsbook, that may be an
American-odds price such as

18
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:56,040
minus one ten. On a pick'em or daily
fantasy platform,

19
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,500
it may be a displayed multiplier, payout
structure,

20
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:02,640
or contest term. Those numbers are not
interchangeable.

21
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:05,370
Write down what the platform actually
shows,

22
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:09,380
and check the current rules and final
entry screen because promotions,

23
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:13,960
combinations, limits, grading rules, and
payouts can change.

24
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:17,860
Here is the speed trick: compare exact
matches first.

25
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:20,260
Same player. Same stat.

26
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:22,540
Same direction. Same line.

27
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:24,180
Nearly the same timestamp.

28
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,630
When all five match, the attached price or
payout context becomes

29
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:31,720
easier to compare. When one field differs,

30
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:34,100
flag the row instead of forcing a winner.

31
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:36,820
A moved line may be useful information,

32
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,680
but it is not a clean apples-to-apples
comparison.

33
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:43,320
Now add Propeller as the evidence layer.

34
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:48,220
Propeller does not automatically compare
every sportsbook or platform for you.

35
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:52,230
Open the current Analyzer, find the same
player and stat when

36
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:55,700
that slate is available, and confirm the
displayed line.

37
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:58,720
Then read the direction and confidence
together.

38
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:03,200
Confidence is directional signal strength
for that displayed line.

39
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:07,150
It is not a guaranteed outcome or a
calibrated chance of

40
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:11,660
winning. After the headline signal,
inspect the available context.

41
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:15,960
Look for role, injuries, matchup, recent
form,

42
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:20,910
game environment, market context, and any
missing data the analysis discloses.

43
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:25,210
No-vig market probability may appear as
one signal where data is

44
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:28,460
available, but it is still a
market-implied estimate,

45
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:30,900
not the true probability of the outcome.

46
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:35,470
A strong-looking price does not repair
stale news or a mismatched

47
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:40,010
line. Before you finish, refresh the tabs
and stamp the time

48
00:02:40,020 --> 00:02:43,400
again. Check player status and event
status.

49
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:48,250
Confirm that the line, price, multiplier,
and rules still match your

50
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:52,300
notes. If the platform changed the line
while you were researching,

51
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:53,900
move it to a new row.

52
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:58,110
Do not silently reuse the earlier
Propeller signal for a different

53
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:02,340
threshold. Your final row should answer
three questions:

54
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:04,460
Is this the exact same prop?

55
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:06,600
Are the source and terms current?

56
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:09,400
Does the evidence still apply to this
line?

57
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:13,400
If any answer is no, the correct output is
no action.

58
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:15,860
Passing is not a failed comparison.

59
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:19,360
It is the workflow protecting you from a
false match.

60
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:23,340
Use Propeller as a research and analytics
tool,

61
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:26,560
not as a sportsbook or an automatic odds
screen.

62
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:31,440
Open the free Analyzer at propellerpicks
dot com slash analyzer,

63
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:34,960
keep your line ledger beside it, and make
your own decision.

64
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:40,230
Use limits, verify current terms, and
remember that no model signal

65
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:41,700
guarantees an outcome.

